{
  "title": "Letter Combinations of a Phone Number",
  "description": "On an old phone keypad each digit from 2 to 9 stands for a few letters:\n\n| Digit | Letters | Digit | Letters |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `2` | `abc` | `6` | `mno` |\n| `3` | `def` | `7` | `pqrs` |\n| `4` | `ghi` | `8` | `tuv` |\n| `5` | `jkl` | `9` | `wxyz` |\n\nGiven a string `digits`, return every string of letters it could spell, choosing one letter for each digit in order. Return the strings in ascending alphabetical order. If `digits` is empty, return an empty list.\n\n### Constraints\n\n- `0 <= digits.size() <= 6`\n- `digits` contains only the characters `2` to `9`.\n- Inputs are passed as function arguments and the answer is returned from the function. Do not read from standard input or print the result.\n",
  "reference": "vector<string> letter_combinations(string digits) {\n    const vector<string> keys = {\"abc\", \"def\", \"ghi\", \"jkl\", \"mno\", \"pqrs\", \"tuv\", \"wxyz\"};\n    vector<string> result;\n    if (digits.empty()) {\n        return result;\n    }\n    result.push_back(\"\");\n    for (char digit : digits) {\n        vector<string> next;\n        for (const string& prefix : result) {\n            for (char letter : keys[digit - '2']) {\n                next.push_back(prefix + letter);\n            }\n        }\n        result = next;\n    }\n    return result;\n}\n",
  "template": "vector<string> letter_combinations(string digits) {\n    // Return every spelling of digits, in alphabetical order.\n    return {};\n}\n",
  "tags": [
    "Strings",
    "Hash Tables",
    "Backtracking"
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "test_number": 0,
      "explanation": "The digit 2 can be a, b, or c and the digit 3 can be d, e, or f. Pairing each choice for 2 with each choice for 3 gives 3 x 3 = 9 strings."
    },
    {
      "test_number": 1,
      "explanation": "A single 2 can only spell one of its three letters."
    }
  ],
  "tests": [
